Thinking process (2 tools used)
Short answer: I can't predict business outcomes, and anyone who gives you a confident percentage is guessing. But I can give a grounded assessment based on how the product and its own GTM plan are actually designed — and the honest read is that "only founder-led sales" is a poor fit for how Comply-Quick is built to be sold. The product is architected for product-led growth (PLG), not high-touch founder sales.
The core mismatch
The GTM plan explicitly names a "Free-First Approach" — free scans as the main CTA, embeddable badges, public score pages, marketplace templates, and partner referrals as the distribution engine. go-to-market-plan.md:23-27 The entire conversion funnel is self-serve: frictionless free scan → "aha" moment → self-serve upgrade → expansion via seats/overage/marketplace. go-to-market-plan.md:35-39 Founder-led sales is barely present — it appears only as "recruit 10 seed agency partners" and outbound to regulated SMBs. go-to-market-plan.md:63-68
Why price points make founder-only sales hard
The economics work against high-touch selling for most of the catalog. Solo is $29/mo and Agency $99/mo. pricing.ts:72-93 At those ACVs, a founder's time spent on 1:1 sales calls almost never pays back — these tiers must be sold by the product itself. Only the Enterprise tier at $299/mo (with "Contact Sales" as its CTA) is a plausible founder-led sales motion, and even that is low for a true enterprise sales cycle. PricingPlans.tsx:60-73
Where founder-led sales does fit
Founder-led selling is genuinely valuable for two segments the plan calls out:
•	Seed agency partners — recruiting the first ~10 agencies with lifetime discounts is exactly the kind of relationship-driven work a founder should do, and agencies then become a distribution multiplier (each resells to many clients). go-to-market-plan.md:63-68
•	Regulated-vertical SMB / Enterprise — the ICP explicitly enters "via demos and inbound leads," which is founder-led sales territory. go-to-market-plan.md:5-9
The honest assessment
•	If "founder-led sales only" means the founder does everything manually and there is no PLG/self-serve engine running: likelihood of success is low. You'd be starving the exact channels the product was built around (free-scan funnel, badges, marketplace, referrals, SEO) and spending founder time on sub-$100 ACV deals that can't repay it.
•	If "founder-led sales" means the founder personally drives GTM in the early days while the self-serve PLG funnel runs underneath: that's actually the healthiest early-stage model, and the likelihood is moderate-to-reasonable — but that is really "founder-led GTM," not "founder-led sales as the only channel."
The plan's own KPI targets (NPS ≥ 40, 60% 30-day retention, 20–30% trial→paid, 100k/mo organic traffic, LTV:CAC ≥ 10:1) are explicitly labeled business outcomes that are not guaranteed — the codebase builds the enabling instrumentation, not the results. BUILD_PLAN.md:109-111
Biggest risks to a founder-only motion
•	Two of the strongest PLG surfaces aren't finished. The token-gated free-scan funnel — the primary top-of-funnel acquisition path — is still in the README's "Still Need to Implement" list. Without it, the self-serve engine that's supposed to reduce reliance on founder selling is incomplete. go-to-market-plan.md:35-39
•	Low-ACV tiers can't sustain a human sales cost. Self-serve conversion must carry Solo/Agency; a founder can't. pricing.ts:72-93
Bottom line: success depends far more on whether the PLG funnel actually works (free-scan → activation → self-serve upgrade) than on founder selling. Founder-led sales should be a wedge for agency partners and enterprise deals layered on top of PLG — not the sole channel. Relying on it exclusively contradicts the product's own design and pricing.
